    slug: 'free-vpn-passive-income-app-residential-proxy-scam',
    description:
      'A free VPN app, a "get paid for your unused internet" passive-income app, or a software development kit bundled quietly inside an otherwise ordinary mobile app enrolls the installing device — a phone, router, smart TV, digital picture frame, or even a connected vehicle system — into a residential proxy network, routing anonymous internet traffic through the owner\'s home IP address under consent buried in unread terms of service. Criminals then rent access to that IP address to mask illegal activity, including credential-stuffing attacks and fraud, which means the device owner\'s own internet connection, not the criminal\'s, is what shows up in a platform\'s fraud-detection logs or, in serious cases, in a law-enforcement inquiry. The FBI\'s Internet Crime Complaint Center warned in a March 2026 advisory that this differs from device-specific botnet malware like BADBOX-infected streaming boxes because it frequently starts with software the owner installed on purpose, sometimes a privacy tool they already trusted, and named five common paths in: proxy code bundled into mobile app SDKs, deceptive free VPN services, pre-compromised IoT hardware sold new, malware hidden inside pirated movies or games, and "bandwidth-sharing" apps marketed as easy passive income. The FBI recommends avoiding free VPN services and unfamiliar streaming devices, downloading apps only from official app stores, keeping device and router firmware updated, and watching home network activity for outbound traffic that doesn\'t match anything the household actually uses.',
