    name: 'Fake Social Media Account Recovery Service Scam',
    slug: 'fake-social-media-account-recovery-service-scam',
    description:
      'Someone locked out of a hacked Instagram, Facebook, or X account — often after a phishing message or a fake copyright complaint took it over in the first place — searches online or posts publicly asking for help, and is contacted by a stranger claiming to be a professional "account recovery specialist," an ethical hacker, or even a fellow victim who got their own account back this way. The self-styled recovery service asks for an upfront fee, commonly around $100, to restore access; once paid, a second charge follows for "software" needed to "complete the task," then a third for something else entirely, with each new fee justified by a story about why the last payment wasn\'t enough — and in one case documented by the Better Business Bureau, the same escalating pattern also extended into the scammer asking for banking information, a CashApp login, and a Social Security number under the guise of identity verification, sometimes backed by a fraudulent IRS.gov-branded page. No payment ever restores the account, because in many documented cases the "recovery service" contacting the victim is the same person who hacked it, or is working with them. The BBB\'s consumer alert states plainly that a person should never pay to recover a social media account, since every major platform — Meta, X, and others — provides its own account-recovery process free of charge through the app or its official help pages, and any third party charging a fee to "restore" an account, especially one that found the victim rather than the other way around, should be reported to BBB.org/ScamTracker rather than paid.',
