    slug: 'georgia-avant-interventional-psychiatry-medicaid-fraud',
    description: `Avant Interventional Psychiatry billed the Georgia Medicaid program for psychiatric services as though they had been personally performed by Dr. Okah Anyokwu, when the services were actually delivered by other Avant staff — some of whom were not licensed or enrolled as Medicaid providers at all, meaning the state should never have been billed for their work under any provider's name. Georgia Attorney General Chris Carr's Medicaid Fraud and Patient Protection Division opened the investigation after receiving a referral from a private citizen and one of the state's Care Management Organizations, and Dr. Anyokwu cooperated substantially once the office made contact. The matter was resolved through a civil settlement announced June 23, 2026 as part of Georgia's participation in that year's National Health Care Fraud Takedown, under which Avant and Dr. Anyokwu agreed to pay $375,000 back to the Georgia Medicaid program without admitting liability. "If you steal from our Medicaid program, you will be held accountable and forced to pay back all the money you stole," Carr said. The case is a reminder that Medicaid billing under a licensed provider's name is only valid when that specific provider actually performed or directly supervised the service, and that patients, staff, or referral partners who suspect services are being billed under the wrong provider's credentials can report it to the Georgia Attorney General's Medicaid Fraud and Patient Protection Division.`,
