    description: `Vidal Roquez of Providence, Rhode Island, served as the in-person cash collector for a "grandparent scam" ring that phoned senior citizens across Rhode Island and Massachusetts, convincing more than a dozen of them that a grandchild had been arrested or been in a car accident and urgently needed bail or legal-fee money — while pressuring each victim not to tell any other family member, so no one could call the grandchild directly and expose the lie. Once a victim agreed to pay, Roquez personally drove to their home, including one victim's house in Little Compton, and collected the cash in person rather than routing it through a wire transfer or gift cards that a bank or retailer might flag. According to the U.S. Attorney's Office for the District of Rhode Island, Roquez pleaded guilty on December 14, 2023, to conspiracy to commit wire fraud, and on May 3, 2024, was sentenced to 12 months and one day in federal prison, ordered to pay $130,300 in restitution to his victims, and required to complete 60 hours of community service meeting with senior citizen organizations to describe how the scheme worked and how older adults can protect themselves. The case was investigated by Homeland Security Investigations and follows a string of related grandparent-scam prosecutions the U.S. Attorney's Office for Rhode Island has brought against other members of the same conspiracy. Because this version of the scam relies on secrecy and speed, the single most effective defense is refusing both: hang up, and call the grandchild — or another family member — directly at a number already saved in your phone before sending or handing over any money, no matter how convincing or urgent the caller sounds.`,
