    description: `Select Motor Company, LLC, a Missouri used car dealership, sold vehicles to consumers — in at least nine cases detailed in the state's lawsuit, with some buyers paying more than $18,000 — without providing the certificate of title required by Missouri law at the time of sale, leaving buyers who had paid in full unable to legally register, insure with full ownership rights, or resell the cars they thought they owned. The Missouri Attorney General's Office sued the dealership, alleging violations of Section 301.210 of Missouri law, which governs transfer of vehicle titles, and of the Missouri Merchandising Practices Act's ban on unfair and deceptive business practices, after consumers who had paid thousands of dollars found themselves unable to obtain clear legal ownership of their vehicles months after driving them off the lot. The lawsuit asks the court to declare the affected buyers the rightful owners and order the dealership to produce valid titles, to permanently bar Select Motor Company from further sales until it can comply with state titling law, to award full restitution to defrauded consumers, and to impose civil penalties. "Whether it's a young family buying a car for work or a retiree relying on fixed income, no one should be left empty-handed by a business that refuses to play by the rules," the Attorney General's office said in announcing the suit. Buyers of a used vehicle in Missouri should insist on receiving the signed certificate of title at the time of sale — not a promise that it's "on the way" — and can verify a vehicle's title status directly through the Missouri Department of Revenue before paying a dealer in full.`,
