    description: `Gaylen Dean Rust of Layton, Utah ran Rust Rare Coin, a well-known Salt Lake City coin shop, while secretly operating what federal prosecutors called a fraudulent silver trading program beginning at least as early as 2008. Rust told investors he had access to a proprietary trading algorithm that let him capitalize on fluctuations in the price of silver bullion, promised average annual returns of 20% to 25% with almost no risk, and claimed he stored millions of dollars in physical silver on investors' behalf at Brink's Global Services. In reality, according to the SEC, CFTC, and Utah Division of Securities, there was no trading program at all — Rust used incoming investor money to pay off earlier investors in classic Ponzi fashion and to cover his own personal and business expenses, eventually taking in more than $200 million from roughly 500 to 568 investors across 16 states before the scheme collapsed in November 2018. Rust's ex-wife Denise Rust and son Joshua Rust were also charged for their roles helping move and launder the fraudulent proceeds; Denise Rust pleaded guilty to money laundering and was sentenced to 18 months in prison. Gaylen Rust pleaded guilty in December 2021 to conspiracy to commit wire fraud, conspiracy to commit money laundering, and securities fraud, and on March 8, 2022 a federal judge sentenced him to 19 years in prison — the longest fraud sentence ever handed down in Utah's federal district — and ordered him to pay more than $153 million in restitution to his victims. A court-appointed receiver has since distributed tens of millions of dollars back to victims, including a $5.9 million distribution the Utah Division of Securities announced in December 2024 that brought total restitution paid out to nearly $30 million. Utah securities regulators emphasize that anyone offering a "trading program" or fund with guaranteed high returns and "almost no risk" should be treated with extreme skepticism, and that investors can and should verify for free, before sending any money, whether a person or firm is actually licensed to sell securities or commodities in Utah through the Division of Securities' license lookup — Rust never was.`,
