    slug: 'ohio-northwest-capital-toledo-ponzi-scheme',
    description: `Northwest Capital LLC, a Toledo-based investment intermediary, spent roughly a decade from 2011 to 2021 raising money from hundreds of Ohio investors by promising to route their cash into accounts-receivable financing for outside businesses, when in fact firm insiders were placing investor money into companies they already knew were failing and using falsified, inaccurate financial documents to keep both banks and investors in the dark about the firm's true condition, according to the Ohio Attorney General's office. State officials say the scheme ultimately involved more than 700 separate investments from at least 200 investors and roughly $72 million in losses before it unraveled. Following a joint investigation by the Ohio Department of Commerce's Division of Securities and the Attorney General's Bureau of Criminal Investigation, several former Northwest Capital insiders were charged and pleaded guilty in Lucas County: former investment adviser Doug Miller admitted to six felonies and was sentenced to seven years in prison; former investment manager James Delverne pleaded guilty to eleven felonies; former chief compliance officer John Walters pleaded guilty to five felony counts and was sentenced to two years; and firm executive Richard Scheich pleaded guilty to five felonies, was sentenced to four years, and was ordered to pay more than $3.9 million in restitution to victims plus over $8 million to defrauded banks. Nancy Rathbun, wife of firm manager Gary Rathbun (who died before trial), pleaded guilty to a related money-laundering count and received two years of community control. Ohio Attorney General Dave Yost's office points to the case as a reminder that investors should independently confirm — through the Ohio Division of Securities or the SEC's EDGAR and FINRA's BrokerCheck systems — that an intermediary firm's underlying investments and financial statements are what the sales pitch claims, rather than relying on years of an adviser's local reputation or an unbroken track record of on-time payments, which is exactly what a Ponzi structure is built to produce until it collapses.`,
